                </nav>
            </aside>

            <!-- Content -->
            <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">

                <!-- Page Header -->
                <div class="mb-12 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-gray-400 mb-4">
                        <a href="/" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Home</a>
                        <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-gray-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
                        <span class="text-white">Changelog</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
                        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-emerald-500/10 flex items-center justify-center text-emerald-400">
                            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 8v4l3 3m6-3a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white">Changelog</h1>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-400 max-w-3xl">
                        Release history for soli-proxy. Binaries for Linux (amd64, arm64) and macOS (arm64) are attached to every
                        <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/soli-proxy/releases" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-400 hover:text-emerald-300 transition-colors">GitHub release</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.6.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.6.0</h2>
                        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">latest</span>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">An upgraded soli reaches running apps</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                On restart, an adopted app still running a binary replaced since it started keeps serving, then is redeployed blue-green onto the new one.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A warning when systemd would kill the apps</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                A unit with a <code>KillMode</code> other than <code>process</code> kills every app with the proxy; the proxy now says so at startup, with the fix.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps that were up stay up</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                An app found running but not adoptable is started again at once instead of being left asleep; only apps with nothing running wait for their first request.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.5.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.5.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps start on their first request after a restart</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                An app that was not running when the proxy starts, and may sleep, is left asleep and woken by its first request instead of starting with all the others: after a reboot, only the visited sites start. Running apps are adopted as before, and apps with <code>idle_timeout = 0</code> start right away.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Dev certificates cover sleeping apps</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The <code>.test</code> fallback certificate listed only running apps, so a sleeping app&rsquo;s first HTTPS request failed before it could wake it.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.4.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.4.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Refuse AI, SEO and scanner crawlers</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code>[bots] block_agents = ["ai-training", "scanners"]</code> answers a 403 to the crawlers that say who they are: presets for AI training crawlers, AI assistants, SEO tools and scanners, or your own substrings. Search engines pass.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Ban scanners on their first probe</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                With <code>traps = true</code>, a client asking for <code>/.env</code>, <code>/wp-login.php</code> or <code>/.git/</code> is banned from every site for an hour; <code>max_404_per_minute</code> does the same for a stream of 404s. A page&rsquo;s own requests never ban anyone, so no site can get your visitors banned.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">On and off, globally or per site</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code>[bots] enabled</code> in <code>config.toml</code>, and <code>[bots] enabled</code> in a site&rsquo;s <code>app.infos</code>, which wins both ways. <code>soli-proxy bots</code> lists the bans, <code>soli-proxy bots unban &lt;ip&gt;</code> lifts one, and the TUI journals each ban.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">User agents in the request log</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code>log_endpoints</code> lines carry the <code>user_agent</code>, to see who is there before refusing anyone.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.3.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.3.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Maintenance windows that end by themselves</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code>soli-proxy maintenance on shop.example.com --for 30m -m &quot;&hellip;&quot;</code> (or <code>--until</code>, or <code>all</code>), <code>off</code> and <code>status</code>; <code>for_secs</code> and <code>until</code> on the admin API. The proxy reopens the site when the end passes, and <code>Retry-After</code> counts down to it.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A maintenance page worth showing</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The built-in page names the app (<code>display_name</code> in <code>app.infos</code>), shows when it is back in the visitor&rsquo;s time zone, speaks French or English after the browser, follows dark mode and reloads itself once the site is back. An app can bring its own as <code>public/maintenance.html</code>, with its stylesheet and images in <code>public/maintenance/</code>, served while it is closed.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white"><code>M</code> in the TUI</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                On an app of the Apps screen: close it for a duration with a message, or reopen it. Closed apps show <code>◆ maintenance</code> and their end time in the dashboard, and the journal records each window.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white"><code>-c</code> before a subcommand is honoured</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code>soli-proxy -c /etc/soli-proxy/proxy.conf deploy shop</code> used to read <code>./proxy.conf</code>: each subcommand&rsquo;s own default won.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.2.2 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.2.2</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The errors screen shows more than the last few hundred requests</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                It re-read only the last 256&nbsp;KB of the log, a few hundred lines on a busy proxy, so older 5xx and 404s were missing while the header counted them. The TUI now scans the last 16&nbsp;MB once, then follows what is appended, rotation included.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.2.1 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.2.1</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Memory on the TUI dashboard</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                What the apps hold in memory, and the host&rsquo;s used, total and available memory, in the strip and in a panel with a bar; the apps screen shows the total too. The error rate is now the share of 5xx responses.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">404s on the errors screen, and when each app last had a request</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The errors screen lists 404s next to the 5xx; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">f</code> shows all, 5xx only or 404 only, and in 404 mode ranks the missing URLs asked for most. The apps list gains a <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">last</code> column: the time since each app&rsquo;s last request.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps sleep after an hour under --dev</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                1.2.0 kept every app awake under <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--dev</code>. Unset, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">idle_timeout</code> is now an hour there (15 minutes otherwise, unchanged).
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.2.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.2.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-06</span>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">Apps now sleep after 15 minutes without a request. Pin the ones that run cron jobs or background workers with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">idle_timeout = 0</code> before upgrading, or keep the old behaviour with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[apps] idle_timeout = 0</code>.</p>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps sleep after 15 minutes idle by default</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[apps] idle_timeout</code> defaults to 900 seconds (never under <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--dev</code>); an explicit value still wins and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">0</code> turns it off. An app with a request still open is never idle: a streaming response or a WebSocket keeps it awake however long it lasts, and the clock starts when the last one ends.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Slow downloads no longer fill the proxy&rsquo;s memory</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The HTTP/1 server read a response from the backend as fast as the backend sent it, whatever the client&rsquo;s speed: a 120&nbsp;MB download to a slow client sat in memory within a second. It now reads only as fast as the client takes it (+3.7&nbsp;MB instead of +80&nbsp;MB measured); fast clients are as fast as before.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.1.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.1.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-05</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A TUI that shows the proxy at work</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The dashboard draws each active app as a branch of the proxy, with packets travelling down it as densely as it gets requests, red for 5xx, a still dotted line when it is asleep. Deploys unroll their stages under their app as they happen, wake-ups read &ldquo;awake in 1.0 s&rdquo;, and a journal on the right tells what just happened. The apps screen sorts by traffic and shows both slots of the selected app, with the traffic moving from one to the other at the switch; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">D</code>, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">R</code> and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">L</code> deploy, restart and open the logs. Motion repaints only while something moves; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">m</code> turns it off. Below 140 columns the screens become tabs and everything fits 80&times;24.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Deploy stages, sleeps and wake-ups on the event stream</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/api/v1/events/apps</code> now emits each step of a deploy as it happens (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">DeployStage</code>: start, health, switch, drain, done or failed), and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Asleep</code> and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Waking</code> for scale to zero. Existing clients only see new event types.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">No second proxy next to a systemd one</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Started by hand, in the foreground or with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">-d</code>, while systemd runs a proxy on the same ports, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">soli-proxy</code> now refuses with the unit, its PID and the <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">systemctl</code> commands to use. It used to share :80 and :443 silently through SO_REUSEPORT and fight the managed one over the apps.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The TUI shows the proxy&rsquo;s uptime, and the right setting for error details</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The dashboard&rsquo;s uptime was the TUI&rsquo;s own. The errors screen pointed at <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">proxy.conf</code> for <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">log_endpoints</code>, which lives in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">config.toml</code>; it now also shows 5xx per app without any setting.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.0.6 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.0.6</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-05</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-400 border border-amber-500/20">perf</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">gzip compression is 40% faster on large pages</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                gzip now runs on the zlib-rs backend, 1.7&times; faster than miniz_oxide at the same level. The encoder is also no longer flushed at every pause of the backend &mdash; between nearly every socket read &mdash; but once it has been quiet for 10&nbsp;ms, so a large page compresses in fewer, larger blocks: smaller and faster, while a streamed page still arrives at most 10&nbsp;ms late. A 15.6&nbsp;MB page went from 153&nbsp;ms to 95&nbsp;ms.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">zstd is preferred when the client accepts several codings</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The default <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">algorithms</code> becomes <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[&quot;zstd&quot;, &quot;br&quot;, &quot;gzip&quot;]</code>: on HTML, zstd at level 3 is as small as brotli 4 or gzip 5 in a quarter to a third of the time. Chrome, Edge and Firefox get zstd; Safari still gets brotli. A config that sets <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">algorithms</code> keeps its order.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.0.5 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.0.5</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-04</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The TUI tells a sleeping app from a stopped one</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                An app put to sleep by scale to zero (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">idle_timeout</code>) read as Stopped, like one that stays down until someone acts. It now shows as <strong>Sleeping</strong>, in purple (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">◐ sleep</code> on the dashboard): it comes back on the next request. The admin API reports it as <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">"asleep": true</code> in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/api/v1/app-metrics</code>.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">No more false health-check failures after an app goes to sleep</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The health check could probe an app the idle reaper was stopping, and logged <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Health check failed … (1/3 before failover)</code> after every sleep. A probe that raced an intentional stop is now ignored, and a process that exits because it was asked to is logged at info rather than as a warning.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.0.4 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.0.4</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-03</span>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">1.0.3 was tagged but never released: its CI failed on a race in a new test. 1.0.4 is the same code with the test fixed.</p>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feat</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The TUI shows which Soli each app runs, and since when</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The apps screen has two new columns, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Soli</code> and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Up</code>, and the detail panel gives the start date. The version is the one of the binary the process actually runs. After <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">soli</code> is upgraded in place, an app that has not restarted still runs the old binary: its version shows as <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">2.14.0!</code> in yellow until a restart picks up the new one.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A failed health check says why</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The warning read <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">error sending request for url (…/up)</code> whatever happened. It now carries the cause, so a wedged app (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">operation timed out</code>) reads differently from one that exited (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">Connection refused</code>).
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">The sites watcher works with a relative sites directory</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                With the default <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">./sites</code>, a site added, removed or renamed went unnoticed until the proxy restarted, with nothing logged. It is picked up again.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.0.2 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.0.2</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-01</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Upgrading from 0.35 no longer quarantines its apps</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                0.35 kept no record of the apps it started, and left them running when it stopped. 1.0.0 could not prove they were its own, refused to touch them and quarantined every app whose port they held. A leftover is now recognised by what it is &mdash; the leader of its own session, running as the app&rsquo;s user, from the app&rsquo;s site directory, with the app&rsquo;s start program &mdash; stopped, and the app starts afresh. Already hit it? Restart the proxy with 1.0.2, or stop the leftover <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">soli serve</code> processes and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">touch &lt;site&gt;/restart.txt</code>.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A burst of logins is no longer turned away on a small machine</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                1.0.0 answered <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">503</code> to a Basic-auth check that waited more than a second for a bcrypt slot, which a few simultaneous logins on a two-core box could hit. A check now waits up to 10&nbsp;s, and a flood is bounded by the length of the queue instead: 16 waiting checks per slot, then <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">503</code> at once.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 1.0.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v1.0.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-10-01</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-red-500/10 text-red-400 border border-red-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A full security audit, fixed</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                bcrypt ran on the request workers, so some forty wrong passwords a second froze every site on the proxy; it now runs on a bounded pool and answers <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">503</code> when that is full. Hash costs are capped at 13, so a tenant can no longer declare a hash that costs days of CPU per attempt. WebSocket upgrades run the route's Lua scripts, rules match a canonical path (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">//admin</code> and <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/%61dmin</code> no longer slip past <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@auth</code>), and every forwarding header is set by the proxy, never taken from the client.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                                In multi-tenant mode a tenant can no longer hang the proxy through its <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.infos</code>, choose its ports, reach another tenant's container, or serve its pages on someone else's domain. Every dependency advisory is gone: <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">cargo audit --deny warnings</code> is clean.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">performance</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Faster on every path</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                App routing reads a published snapshot instead of taking a global lock five times per request, matched routes borrow their rule instead of cloning it, and TLS sessions resume. Against 0.35.2 on the same machine: <em class="text-white not-italic font-medium">+25%</em> requests per second on a static route, <em class="text-white not-italic font-medium">+34%</em> on an app's domain, and ordinary traffic keeps flowing at 87,000 req/s while wrong passwords flood an <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@auth</code> route, where 0.35.2 managed 30.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps survive a proxy restart</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Stopping, restarting or upgrading the proxy no longer takes every app down. In-flight requests drain for up to <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">shutdown_grace_period</code>, the apps keep running, and the next start adopts each one after checking it is the process it started, launched the same way, on the right port and healthy. <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">soli-proxy check</code> validates the configuration and every site before you restart.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Behind a CDN, and what it sees</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">trusted_proxies</code> (with a Cloudflare preset) and PROXY protocol v1/v2 give the real client to rate limits, logs and allowlists. Every request carries an <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">X-Request-Id</code>, and the access log writes one JSON or combined line per request.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Compression, error pages, maintenance, forward-auth</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Opt-in gzip, brotli and zstd compression; custom HTML error pages per proxy or per app; maintenance mode from the admin API or a <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">maintenance.flag</code> file. <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@forward_auth</code> lets one SSO service such as oauth2-proxy or Authelia gate any route or app.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Upstreams</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                A failed attempt is retried on the next target when that is safe, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">proxy.conf</code> targets get active health checks, and backends can be reached over HTTP/2 and gRPC, with a custom CA, SNI or client certificate, or over a Unix socket. <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">weight:</code>, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">headers { }</code> and regex captures now do what the docs always said.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-400 border border-amber-500/20">upgrading</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Before you upgrade</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">proxy.conf</code> is parsed strictly, hashes above cost 13 must be regenerated, the systemd unit runs as a dedicated user with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">KillMode=process</code>, and the HTTPS listener follows <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[server] bind</code>. The full list is under <em class="text-white not-italic font-medium">Upgrading from 0.35</em> in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">CHANGELOG.md</code>.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.34.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.34.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-09-15</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Scale to zero &mdash; sleep an idle app, wake it on the next request</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Most fleets are mostly idle. On a box hosting thirty small sites a day's traffic typically touches a handful, and every one of the
                                others holds its full runtime in memory for nothing. <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">idle_timeout = 900</code> in
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.infos</code> stops such an app after fifteen quiet minutes and starts it again when
                                someone asks for it. A reaper sweeps every 30&nbsp;s; the stop goes through the same path as
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">soli-proxy stop</code>, so the exit is not read as a crash &mdash; no failover, no quarantine.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                                The next request for one of its domains is <em class="text-white not-italic font-medium">held</em> while the app starts on its current slot and is polled for health,
                                then forwarded as usual: the first visitor waits about a second, everyone else finds it running, and concurrent first requests share
                                one start. A sleeping app keeps its certificate registered and keeps winning over static
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">proxy.conf</code> rules for its domains, exactly as a running one does. The default is
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">0</code> &mdash; never sleep &mdash; which is the right value for anything that works
                                without being asked: cron jobs, background workers, WebSocket rooms, a cache that cannot be rebuilt in a moment.
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[apps] idle_timeout</code> in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">config.toml</code> sets a
                                fleet-wide default; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">_admin</code> never sleeps regardless.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Per-environment sections in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.infos</code></h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                One manifest, two environments. A manifest may carry <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[development]</code> and
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[production]</code> sections, and the proxy's
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--dev</code> flag picks one &mdash; the same flag that already appends
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--dev</code> to an auto-detected Soli start script and registers each app's
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">.test</code> alias. The alternative, a dev copy of the file and a prod copy, is two
                                files nobody diffs until the day they disagree about something that matters.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                                The selected section is applied key by key over the top level, so a key it does not mention keeps the value written above, and a nested
                                table merges into its counterpart rather than replacing it &mdash; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[production.auth.users]</code>
                                adds accounts without discarding the <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">noauth</code> list under
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[auth]</code>. The section that is not selected is dropped unread, so a
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[production]</code> block written for a newer proxy never stops a developer's machine
                                from starting the app. A manifest carrying neither section parses exactly as before.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Discovery says which <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.infos</code> keys it did not understand</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                An unrecognised key was dropped without a word &mdash; <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">worker = 4</code> ran the app with one
                                worker and said nothing &mdash; and the new overlay sections raise the stakes, since a key that lands in the wrong section is ignored just as
                                quietly. Unknown keys are now logged at startup. They stay ignored rather than fatal: refusing the manifest would take a running app off
                                the routing table over a typo.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.33.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.33.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-09-10</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">HTTP Basic Auth per app, in <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.infos</code></h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Apps are routed by the app manager rather than by <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">proxy.conf</code> rules &mdash; static rules for
                                app-managed domains are pruned &mdash; so a route's <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@auth</code> could never protect an app. An app now
                                declares its own, with an <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[auth.users]</code> table of <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">username = "bcrypt hash"</code>
                                entries and an <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[auth] noauth</code> list of paths served without credentials. Auth follows the app's
                                derived domains (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">www.</code>-stripped, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">.test</code> in dev) and any admin-managed
                                alias, and is enforced on WebSocket upgrades as well as plain requests.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                                An <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[auth]</code> section the proxy cannot enforce as written &mdash; an empty hash, a pattern that does not
                                compare literally &mdash; makes the app fail to load rather than come up unprotected.
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">GET /api/v1/apps</code> reports the configured usernames and carve-outs, never the hashes, and the admin UI
                                shows an <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">auth</code> badge on protected apps.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">A contested domain resolves to one app, deterministically</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The host &rarr; app lookup iterated a hash map, so when two apps declared one domain the app it returned could differ from the one
                                actually routed to (which picks by name order). That already misattributed per-app metrics; with per-app auth reading the same
                                lookup it would have meant answering a protected app's traffic with another app's credentials, or none.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.32.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.32.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-09-09</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Exempt paths from a route's Basic Auth</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                A whole domain can be password-protected while the endpoints machines call &mdash; a payment webhook, a health probe &mdash;
                                stay reachable without credentials:
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">app.example.com -&gt; http://app:8080 @auth:admin:$2b$12$... @noauth:/webhooks/stripe,/hooks/*</code>.
                                Entries are comma-separated and each is either an exact path or a prefix ending in
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">*</code> (which also matches the bare prefix). Paths are matched against the URL as the client sent it,
                                before any prefix stripping, so what you write is what you see in the browser.
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@noauth</code> without <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">@auth</code> does nothing. Editable from the admin UI
                                (Routes &rarr; Basic Auth) and the TUI route form, and exposed as <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">auth_exempt</code> on the admin API.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-3">
                                The match is deliberately literal and fails closed: a path carrying percent-encoding or a
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">..</code> segment is never exempt, so a request cannot walk out of the carve-out into a protected path
                                that a normalising backend would resolve differently. Patterns that cannot be compared literally are refused by the admin API
                                and dropped with a warning by the <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">.conf</code> parser, leaving the path protected.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.31.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.31.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-09-04</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Path traversal rejected before routing</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Rules match on the raw path and per-route auth binds to the matched rule, so
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/api/../admin/users</code> could pass an open
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/api/</code> rule and land on a protected route at any backend that
                                normalises. Dot segments in every spelling (literal, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">%2e</code>,
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">..;</code>, <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">..\</code>)
                                and encoded slashes are answered 400. Backends that carry <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">%2F</code>
                                as data can set <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[server] allow_encoded_slash = true</code>.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Multi-tenant mode for untrusted apps</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[apps] multi_tenant = true</code> treats every app as untrusted code:
                                a <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">docker_image</code> is mandatory, every container gets a read-only
                                root, dropped capabilities, no-new-privileges, pid/memory/cpu ceilings and a non-root uid that tenant config cannot weaken, and
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">docker_options</code> is validated against an allowlist. Bind mounts may only be
                                the app's own site directory, passed to docker by its canonical path so a running container cannot race the check with a symlink.
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">docker_network</code> may not join the host or another container in any mode,
                                and the single-tenant denylist now reads docker's syntax (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">-v/:/host</code>,
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--mount type=bind,source=/</code>).
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Admin API hardening</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Mutating requests without <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">X-Api-Key</code> must carry an
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">X-Requested-With</code> header, which an HTML form cannot set.
                                Route password hashes are never returned by the API; a re-submitted entry with an empty hash keeps the existing one, matched by
                                the rule's matcher rather than its position so a whole-table update that deletes or reorders rules cannot hand a route another
                                route's password. Empty admin credentials (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">api_key = ""</code>)
                                count as unset instead of producing a server that rejects everything. Lua hook errors fail closed with a 500.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Apps get their own HOME and an environment allowlist</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                The proxy drops privileges to the app's user but handed the child its own
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">HOME</code> (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">/root</code> under systemd),
                                silently breaking soli's package cache, registry credentials and the Tailwind CLI. It now comes from the passwd entry of the user the
                                app runs as. A short allowlist (<code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">HTTP(S)_PROXY</code>,
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">SSL_CERT_FILE</code>,
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">XDG_CACHE_HOME</code>, the soli toolchain variables) survives the
                                cleared environment, for native and Docker apps alike, so pinned interpreter versions and egress proxies work on a server.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.29.1 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.29.1</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026-07-28</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/20">feature</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Deploy trigger file</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Touching <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">restart.txt</code> at the root of a site triggers a
                                zero-downtime blue/green deploy, so a deploy script can end with
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">touch &lt;site&gt;/restart.txt</code> instead of an SSH-side restart.
                                The file is polled rather than watched, because inotify does not traverse the symlinks that sites usually are. The first poll after
                                startup only records a baseline, so an existing trigger file never redeploys everything on daemon restart. Configurable through
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">[apps].restart_trigger_file</code> and
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">restart_trigger_poll_secs</code>.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">reliability</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Quarantine on failed start</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                An app that fails to start is no longer restarted forever. The health loop previously retried every 30 seconds with no failure cap,
                                leaving a broken deploy flapping indefinitely. The failed slot is now killed and marked failed, <strong class="text-gray-200">the previous
                                slot keeps serving</strong>, and every automatic remediation path skips the app until an explicit deploy. Surfaced as
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">"quarantined": true</code> on the apps API.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-cyan-500/10 text-cyan-400 border border-cyan-500/20">fix</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">systemd unit and Docker entrypoint repaired</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Both shipped deployment paths could never start: the systemd unit invoked a subcommand that does not exist, and the Docker
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">CMD</code> passed a flag that does not exist. Both now use
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--conf</code> and
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">--sites-dir</code>, with a working directory so
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">run/</code> and
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">certs/</code> resolve to the mounted volumes.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- 0.29.0 -->
                <div class="mb-10 reveal-on-scroll">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5 flex-wrap">
                        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-mono">v0.29.0</h2>
                        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">Security hardening</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Streaming request-body limits</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                Body-size limits are enforced on chunked and HTTP/2 bodies by streaming through a limiter and returning
                                <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-white/5 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-mono">413</code>, instead of blanket-rejecting chunked encoding.
                                A body that omits or misreports its length can no longer buffer without bound.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-400 border border-rose-500/20">security</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Header and target validation</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                WebSocket upgrade requests and forwarded headers are guarded against CR/LF injection, and proxy target URLs from configuration and
                                Lua overrides are restricted to safe schemes, closing an SSRF avenue.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2 flex-wrap">
                                <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-semibold bg-purple-500/10 text-purple-400 border border-purple-500/20">performance</span>
                                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white">Shared connection pool, faster routing</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                                All listeners share a single connection pool with a per-host idle cap. Host matching is a single case-insensitive scan again,
                                replacing an optimization that rebuilt a throwaway hash map on every request.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/5 bg-white/[0.02] p-5">
                    <p class="text-gray-400">
                        Full history in
                        <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/soli-proxy/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-400 hover:text-emerald-300 transition-colors">CHANGELOG.md</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
